{
 "summary": "The Lean target says that EVERY group G : Type (with any group structure) is finitely presented and not sofic. That statement is false: the trivial group is finitely presented and satisfies the file's `Sofic` definition, and uncountable groups are not finitely presented. The paper claims that one specific group, the unit group L_{F2}(1,2)^×, is not sofic, which gives the existence of a nonsofic group. The decisive caveat is that the quantifier is ∀ where it should be ∃ (the theorem's own name says 'exists'), so the formal statement does not express the paper's result and is unprovable unless something is inconsistent.",
 "glossary": [
  {"decl": "normalizedHamming", "line": 7, "plain": "d_H(p,q) = |{y : p y ≠ q y}| / |Y| for permutations p, q of a finite set Y", "standard_concept": "normalized Hamming distance on Sym(Y)", "matches_standard": "yes", "note": "If Y is empty, Lean's x/0 = 0 gives distance 0, but PermutationModel requires size > 0, so this case never arises."},
  {"decl": "PermutationModel", "line": 11, "plain": "A map σ : G → Sym(n) with n > 0 and σ(1) = id; σ need not be a homomorphism.", "standard_concept": "candidate sofic approximation map", "matches_standard": "yes", "note": "Requiring σ(1) = id is a harmless normalization."},
  {"decl": "GoodOn", "line": 17, "plain": "σ is (F,ε)-good: d_H(σ(gh), σ(g)σ(h)) < ε for all g, h ∈ F, and d_H(σ(g), id) > 1 − ε for g ∈ F \\ {1}.", "standard_concept": "(F,ε)-sofic approximation", "matches_standard": "yes", "note": ""},
  {"decl": "Sofic", "line": 24, "plain": "For every finite F ⊆ G and every 0 < ε < 1 there is an (F,ε)-good permutation model.", "standard_concept": "sofic group", "matches_standard": "yes", "note": "The definition is stated for arbitrary groups, not only countable ones; it is the standard local definition."}
 ],
